 serum  to different cell lines different cancer  cell lines  and studied the phenotype and genetic  changes within these cells  and what they noticed was that cells  that were given the  older human serum shows pro-metastatic  properties  and so to understand why this was the  case  they looked at the presence of different  metabolites within both the young  and old healthy donor human serum  and what they found was as expected they  saw  decreases in the levels of glutathione  spermadine and glutamine  and alpha-ketoglutarate but they also  saw  consistent increases in the serum of age  donors  and the three metabolites that were  consistently increased were  phosphoenol prorevate which is a  component  of glycolysis quinolinate  and methylmalonic acid and so they  tested each of these individually to see  if any of them on their own  could induce the pro-aggressive effects  seen in these different  cancer cells and they found that the  methyl melanic acid  mma could and say what is  mma so mma is a byproduct of the  metabolism  of branched chain amino acids and  it can be linked to coenzyme a and that  can be converted to succinyl coa  and then that can be fed into the krebs  cycle however this conversion  requires an enzyme that depends on  vitamin b12 as a k  factor and so it's already been pretty  well established that  levels of mma increase in people who  have vitamin b12  deficiency and also vitamin b12 is known  to decrease  during age however they also show that  that isn't likely to be the full  scenario  and so another interesting finding from  this study is the fact that  mma is more effective at causing this  pro-tumerogenic response  at lower concentrations if mma is in a  lipidic structure  because this is transports of mma inside  the cell  so what is mma doing to the cell once  it's inside  well this isn't still fully understood  but what they observed was an  upregulation  of a transcription factor known as sox4  and sox4 is already a marker of poor  prognosis  that contributes to human progression in  a variety of  aggressive cancers and interestingly  sucks for depletion fully abrogated the  ability of mma to promote migratory and  invasive properties  of the cells but it seems likely that  mma is impacting the tgf beta signaling  pathway that is upstream of sox4  to achieve this impact so i'm sure  further studies will elucidate this a  little bit further  but it highlights the importance of  metabolic reprogramming  to the relationship between aging and  cancer  so going on from lipidic structures of  mma  now to functionalized lipid-like  nanoparticles for in-fivo  mrna delivery and base editing so what's  this all about  well many diseases occur due to  a deficiency of a protein for example  hemophilia a can occur due to a lack of  human factor eight  and so in terms of treating these  different diseases it's not trivial just  to be able to  give back a protein neither is it  trivial to be able to edit  the source of the genetic mutation in  the first place  and part of this comes down to the facts  of the difficulty of getting the  materials to the right place  at the right time and so what is  explored in this paper  is the advancement made in the  development of lipids based  nanoparticles  that can carry mrna and so mrna  is the transcript produced from dna  which is the intermediate between dna  and  protein production and so if a cell  receives mrna it can then convert it  into protein  and bypass the the dna stage  and so in the study they screened a  variety of different  functionalized lipid-like nanoparticles  and they found that one of these  compounds  which they referred to as ftd5 was able  to efficiently deliver  long mrnas in this case human factor  8 for the treatment of hemophilia a  and so this was done in mice and so what  they found  was that the delivery of this protein  which enables blood to clot to treat the  symptoms of hemophilia  was able to produce enough of this  factor  within 12 hours to reach 90 percent of  what is seen in normal activity  so as well as this actually being  effective it was also shown that there  was no apparent organ damage within the  mice as well  but the more interesting example that  they used was  a lipid-like nully particle that  contained the components of a base  editor  so they had the mrna encoding the base  editor  and also a single guide rna to target  the base editor  to the desirable sides on the dna  and so this is different so this time  we're not just producing a protein we're  producing a protein which is the base  editor  and the machinery to be able to actually  edit  dna so it's kind of combining two  treatments in one  and this was pretty cool and they show a  high percentage of base editing when  they try to target  the gene pcsk9 the protein encoded by  this gene is important in cholesterol  clearance  and pcsk9 is commonly mutated  and so these sets of experiments  are pretty exciting in terms of  potential therapies in the future  and notably the technology has been  licensed for further clinical  development  so lastly today in this video we're  going to look at  genetically modified mosquitoes that  have been okayed for a first  u.s test flight and so mosquitoes can  carry a variety of different diseases  such as dengue and the most common used  technique  to eliminate outbreaks or bursts of  mosquitoes is by using pesticides and so  not only are pesticides somewhat  ineffective they also have  adverse consequences for other wildlife  and for the environment  but an alternative strategy is to reduce  the rate of reproduction of these  mosquitoes  and one way that you can do that is  through genetic modification  and so there's an english biotech  company known as oxytec  that have developed normal looking male  mosquitoes  that actually contain a gene that they  call a self-limiting gene  that means that when they mate with a  female  any of the female offspring die whilst  only the males  can survive and so also you need the  female  to reproduce to create more offspring  and so if you've only got the males  surviving  you effectively shrink the population  size of these forms of mosquitoes  and the technology that actually enables  this is pretty neat  so this self-limiting gene that they  refer to  is basically a gene that gets expressed  within  only the females and when it gets  expressed  it promotes its own expression and so  you have tons of this one protein within  the cell  and this protein actually binds to the  transcriptional machinery  that basically prevents other genes from  being expressed  and so without all these other essential  genes being expressed  the female larvae actually die before  becoming adults  and so obviously one concern with  genetically modified  organisms is always to safety and  whether or not  the technology can spread to other  organisms but the nature of this  genetically modified  system is such that it is self-limiting  and so it means that it can't establish  in the ecosystem  and it would only target their own  species and moreover  it is environmentally sustainable since  it prevents the use  of chemicals so i guess if all goes to  plan  they will be released in 2021 in florida  keys  so that brings us to the end of these  brief news articles  as i said this is the first time i've  done this i don't know if this is  good bad or whatever but i find it  interesting to  do so that's all i've got to say  so i hope you've learned something and  thanks for listening
 

